help() {
	cat <<"HELP";
NAME

	rrssh - remote recursive ssh

SYNOPSIS

	rrssh [<options>...] <main-command> [--- <rrssh commands>...]

DESCRIPTION

	An automation tool that uses ssh to jump through any number of
	hosts to run commands and transfer files and directories.  Its
	commands are a basic shell-like scripting language provided in
	arguments or in a file.  Execution of the script and file
	transfers will span as many hosts as indicated.  A strong panic
	mecanism will stop the execution at any unexpected failures,
	either external commands exiting with an unhandled non-0 code or
	an internal rrssh failure.

	Rrssh is a bash script that implements a recursive scripting
	language.  Rrssh is, in most part, a bash "kernel" that duplicates
	itself locally or through an ssh connection.  Because of its
	nature, rrssh has an extensive test suite to ensure its
	reliability.

	The only requirements for the target hosts are an old bash version
	and basic utilities.

MAIN COMMANDS

	For the main commands that need a script, the script must be
	provided either with the option --- or -f.

	run

		Run the script.

	check

		Examine the script for syntax errors and other problems
		without running it.

	print [-n <index>] [-lt]

		Prints the script, one token per line.  With -n, only print
		that token.  Tokens are indexed at 0.  With -t, only print the
		amount of tokens.  With -l, do not quote the tokens using
		printf %q.

	pretty-print

		Pretty prints the script for an easier visual analysis.

	walk

		Try going through all the hosts and all the user switchings,
		without running any commands.

	print-kernel

		Duplicates the kernel to stdout.  Send it to shellcheck for
		analysis.

OPTIONS

	-h, --help

		Show this help message.

	--- <commands>...

		Stop reading options and arguments.  The remaining arguments
		will be interpreted as a script.  --- is necessary if the
		script is provided by arguments.

	-f, --file <filepath>

		Provide a file of commands to execute instead of reading from
		the arguments.  If file is "-", then read from stdin.

	-p

		Any command chain that does not exit with 0 will make rrssh
		panic.  Default behavior is to only make the parent scope exit
		non-0.

	-n <index>

		Read the description of the command "print".

	-l, -t

		Read the description of the command "print".

OPTIONS USED BY RRSSH'S TEST SUITE

	--make-command-fail <id>

		Do not use this.  This is intended for the test suite.  Makes
		a command fail to validate the behavior of the error paths.

	--fake-su

		Do not use this.  This is intended for the test suite.  Make
		the su commands not really attempt to switch user to prevent
		passwords from breaking the test suite.

COMMANDS

	In the following, square brackets [ ] may indicate optionnal
	arguments or literal arguments to be provided to rrssh.

	host <destination> [ <commands>... ]
	sudo-host <destination> [ <commands>... ]

		These commands are the main purpose of using rrssh.
		Duplicates the kernel through a single ssh connection and
		execute the sub commands on the remote host.  This command can
		include itself arbitrarily many times to reach any depth.  The
		[ ] variants are scoped.  The : variants takes all the
		commands until the end of the current scope.  The sudo
		variants will run the actual ssh command through sudo.

	su <user> [ <commands>... ]
	sudo-su <user> [ <commands>... ]

		TODO

	command <system-command>
	command [ <argv>... ]
	--- <argv>... ---

		TODO

	true
	false

		Do nothing, with success or failure.

	for <variable> in <values>... [ <commands>... ]
	for <variable> <delim> <values>... <delim> <command>
	for <variable> <delim> <values>... <delim> [ <commands>... ]
	for <variable> --- <values>... --- <command>
	for <variable> --- <values>... --- [ <commands>... ]

		Loop a block of code over some values.  Each time, all
		instances of <variable> in the commands will be substituted.
		Use special symbols to avoid replacing unwanted parts of the
		commands.  You cannot replace syntax elements of the language
		with variables.

	var <variable> <value>

		Create a variable.  The variable is available and affectable
		in the current scope and all child scopes.  You cannot create
		a variable if its name already exists in the current scope.
		Use the hosts' "test" command with rrssh's "command" command
		to compare its value.

	set-var <variable> <value>

		Change the value of a variable.  The variable must have
		previously been created with the var command in the current
		scope or a parent scope.

	if <command> then <command>
			[ elif <command> then <command> ]...
			[ else <command> then <command> ]
			fi

		TODO

pour implémenter les if et les scopes, il faut donner au nouveau
